  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale: \n   We will use a gradient-based optimization method tailored for step functions rather than linear programming. This approach leverages the structure of the step function to guide the optimization more effectively. The key idea is to maximize the L<inf>distance</inf> between consecutive segments while maintaining a fixed sum. This ensures that the peak autocorrelation is handled by controlling the distribution within the sequence. The method involves coordinate-wise updates with adaptive step sizes and periodic global resampling to escape local minima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   Given a sequence of length n=512, we wish to minimize the score defined as 2.0*n*max(convolve(a,a))/sum(a)**2. We will fix the sum to 1000.0 (which is the clip upper bound) to make the optimization scale-invariant. The sequence a has components in [0, 1000]. We compute the gradient of the score with respect to each element by finite-difference approximation:\n\n   \u2207_i = (f(a_{i}+\u03b4) - f(a_{i}-\u03b4)) / (2\u03b4)\n\n   where f(a) = evaluate_sequence(a) (clipping values and normalizing).\n\n   The fixed sum constraint requires that changes to individual elements be compensated globally. We handle this by first perturbing an element and then adjusting the entire sequence so that the sum remains fixed at 1000.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   We will implement a gradient optimization with linesearch. Steps:\n\n   - If a parent sequence is provided, use it as initial guess.\n   - Otherwise, generate a random sequence of length 512.\n   - Normalize initial sequence to sum 1000.\n   - While time budget allows:\n        * Fix sequence length at 512.\n        * Ensure all values are in [0, 1000].\n        * Compute total sum and normalization factor.\n        * For each element in the sequence:\n            * Perturb by a small amount \u03b4 (start with 0.01).\n            * Check constraints and adjust \u03b4 for feasibility.\n            * Use finite differences to approximate gradient.\n            * Update the element using gradient descent with an adaptive learning rate.\n        * Regularly add random offsets to maintain exploration.\n\n   - Final polishing phase to ensure minimal score.\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   We will use \u03b4 = 0.01 for the finite-difference estimate unless the perturbation exceeds [0,1000], then use smaller \u03b4. \n   The learning rate starts at 0.01 and halves every 100 steps.\n   We include periodic global restarts by small Gaussian perturbations (with mean 0 and standard deviation 0.05) to the entire sequence.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   Time estimates: Each evaluation with evaluate_sequence takes approximately O(512*log2(512)) for the FFT convolution, which is around 25 ms. \n   We perform 2*length_of_sequence (for finite differences) evaluations per coordinate update. \n   With 512 coordinates and the linesearch restarting phase, the computations are manageable within 1800 seconds.\n\n   We have a fallback to return the best sequence found, which must be:\n   - Non-negative\n   - Values clipped to [0, 1000]\n   - Sum normalized to 1000\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   After processing, we clamp the sequence to [0, 1000] and use the formula 2.0*n*max(convolve(a,a))/sum(a)**2. \n   The fallback is a valid sequence if no improvement is found.\n\nHere is the implementation:",
